    {
      "id": "strengths",
      "seconds": 20.4,
      "heading": "What each does best",
      "narration": "Each one has its strength. Its inventor saw {APL|@apl} as a tool of thought. The tacit style is central to {J|Jay}. {K|Kay} is small and fast, and popular in finance. {BQN|@bqn} has fewer primitives than {APL|@apl} or {J|Jay}, and less overloading.",
      "lines": [],
      "slide": {
        "type": "points",
        "steps": [
          0,
          0,
          1,
          2,
          3,
          4
        ],
        "items": [
          {
            "text": "APL",
            "sub": "notation as a tool of thought (Iverson, 1979)"
          },
          {
            "text": "J",
            "sub": "tacit programming, all in ASCII"
          },
          {
            "text": "K",
            "sub": "small and fast; kdb+ and q are built on it"
          },
          {
            "text": "BQN",
            "sub": "fewer, less overloaded primitives (Why BQN?)"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "xetal-fit",
      "seconds": 22.8,
      "heading": "Where X_eTaL fits",
      "narration": "So where does {X_eTaL|@xtl} fit. Like {J|Jay} and {K|Kay}, you type {ASCII|ass key}. You {read|reed} it drawn, as in {APL|@apl}, and in {BQN|@bqn}. Its built-ins are mostly words, with one meaning each. Functions are passed as values, so it needs no operators. And its types are checked before it runs.",
